{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779125329,"nonce":"GBBoXyCZlZpZBy0xZm1h+xoOZrA=","message":"## Second Review: table should own interaction; top summaries still imply magic metadata\n\nUser review of the first implementation pass found that the direction is closer, but still too metadata/display oriented in several places.\n\nConcrete issues to address in this slice or immediately after:\n\n- The top summary/readiness cards (`Completed`, `Best loss`, `Selected`, `Model and task`, `Sweep`, `Compute target`, `Next step`) are mostly redundant with the table or with the run-target control. Selected count belongs near the table selection controls, and completion/progress belongs in table columns.\n- Train and Evaluate currently both select training runs for evaluation. That creates two authoritative selection surfaces. Train may allow row selection for local/bulk actions, but Evaluate should own the evaluation-set composer. Transferring a selection from Train to Evaluate is more like a contextual action than a persistent bottom-card CTA.\n- Text such as \"Imported RLRMP runs are grouped as one batch...\" is not a general UX primitive. Some descriptive text may come from a user/agent-authored spec note, but the core UI should not depend on prose that magically appears.\n- \"Sweep\" and \"Protocol\" cannot just be displayed as metadata. They need to be authored interactively through task/scenario/objective/protocol state, mostly in the top pane and its inspectors. Until that exists, the table should not imply that sweeps/protocols are fully configurable here.\n- Run names should eventually come from deterministic summaries of authored sweep choices, with user rename support. The seeded names are fixture/import labels, not a product-level naming system.\n- `checkpoint` is vague. The table needs a concrete checkpoint/progress column, e.g. current batch and latest checkpoint batch, live-updated from the worker/server when available.\n- Per-row `Provenance` details dirty the table. Move this to an info/details button opening a bottom-pane-scoped inspector/modal.\n- Metric columns such as velocity RMSE, hold drift, and peak velocity are task-specific imported metrics, not universal training-run fields. Created `e5a1e6c` to track task-aware training metrics derived from metric/analysis specs.\n- Loss function, optimizer, batch count, and other training protocol controls disappeared in this slice. Long term, objective/loss authoring belongs in the top structural/scenario workspace (`5378a5d`), while optimizer/batch/checkpoint/compute settings belong in a concise training protocol editor, not in a giant metadata panel.\n\nImmediate refinement direction:\n\n- Remove the top summary/readiness card grid for Train/Evaluate.\n- Move selected count into the run table toolbar.\n- Convert rows to an actual table-like layout with a single header and sortable columns, default sorted by loss ascending.\n- Replace row `Provenance` disclosure with an info/details icon that opens an in-pane detail overlay.\n- Change checkpoint display to `Latest checkpoint` / `Progress` wording instead of a vague `checkpoint` pill.\n- Keep Train as run review/bulk action surface; Evaluate owns the evaluation selection composer.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}